WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.920
Near the Moon's south pole, some crater floors have not seen sunlight in billions of years.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.520
What could be hiding in the dark?

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:22.780
On October 9, 2009, NASA's LCROSS mission crashed its spent rocket stage into one of those craters, Cabeus.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:29.190
In the plume of dust, it found water: about 155 kilograms.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.470
Orbiters study the poles without landing.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:41.760
A laser measures the height of the ground. A neutron counter senses hydrogen, a sign of ice.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.650
A thermometer found some of the coldest places ever measured in the solar system.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:55.010
The Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has mapped the Moon since 2009.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:02.520
Now compare your design. Water there means drinking water, air and rocket fuel for explorers.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.180
<v Franchesca>Where there is water, there are questions. Maybe even answers.

